    <main class="lesson-content">
        <article class="lesson-article">
            <LessonHeader 
                LessonNumber="6"
                Title="Querying with LINQ"
                Duration="21 min"
                Level="Intermediate" />
            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Filtering with Where</h2>
                    <p>Use <code>Where</code> to apply filters that translate to SQL WHERE clauses; combine expressions for complex filters.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Simple filter
var expensiveProducts = await context.Products
    .Where(p => p.Price > 100)
    .ToListAsync();

// Multiple conditions
var activeExpensive = await context.Products
    .Where(p => p.Price > 100 && p.IsActive)
    .ToListAsync();

// String operations
var laptops = await context.Products
    .Where(p => p.Name.Contains("Laptop"))
    .ToListAsync();</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Sorting</h2>
                    <p>Use <code>OrderBy</code> and <code>ThenBy</code> for sorting; EF Core translates these to ORDER BY in SQL.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Ascending
var byName = await context.Products
    .OrderBy(p => p.Name)
    .ToListAsync();

// Descending
var byPriceDesc = await context.Products
    .OrderByDescending(p => p.Price)
    .ToListAsync();

// Multiple sort criteria
var sorted = await context.Products
    .OrderBy(p => p.Category.Name)
    .ThenByDescending(p => p.Price)
    .ToListAsync();</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Pagination</h2>
                    <p>Implement pagination with <code>Skip</code> and <code>Take</code>; always apply a deterministic order before paging.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">int page = 1;
int pageSize = 10;

var pagedProducts = await context.Products
    .OrderBy(p => p.Name)
    .Skip((page - 1) * pageSize)
    .Take(pageSize)
    .ToListAsync();

var totalCount = await context.Products.CountAsync();</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Projection with Select</h2>
                    <p>Use <code>Select</code> to project only the columns you need (more efficient) and to map results to DTOs directly in the query.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Select specific columns (more efficient)
var productNames = await context.Products
    .Select(p => p.Name)
    .ToListAsync();

// Project to DTO
var productDtos = await context.Products
    .Select(p => new ProductDto
    {
        Id = p.Id,
        Name = p.Name,
        Price = p.Price,
        CategoryName = p.Category.Name
    })
    .ToListAsync();

// Anonymous type
var summary = await context.Products
    .Select(p => new { p.Name, p.Price })
    .ToListAsync();</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Grouping and Aggregates</h2>
                    <p>Group results for reporting and use aggregate methods (Count, Sum, Average) which translate to SQL aggregates.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Group by category
var byCategory = await context.Products
    .GroupBy(p => p.CategoryId)
    .Select(g => new
    {
        CategoryId = g.Key,
        Count = g.Count(),
        TotalValue = g.Sum(p => p.Price),
        AveragePrice = g.Average(p => p.Price)
    })
    .ToListAsync();

// Aggregates
var maxPrice = await context.Products.MaxAsync(p => p.Price);
var minPrice = await context.Products.MinAsync(p => p.Price);
var count = await context.Products.CountAsync();
var sum = await context.Products.SumAsync(p => p.Stock);</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Query vs Method Syntax</h2>
                    <p>Method syntax (fluent LINQ) is most common in EF Core, but query syntax can be useful for complex SQL-like queries—both get translated to SQL.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Method syntax (more common)
var products = await context.Products
    .Where(p => p.Price > 100)
    .OrderBy(p => p.Name)
    .ToListAsync();

// Query syntax (SQL-like)
var products = await (
    from p in context.Products
    where p.Price > 100
    orderby p.Name
    select p
).ToListAsync();</code></pre>
                </section>

                <LessonTakeaways>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Where filters, OrderBy sorts, Skip/Take paginates.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Use Select to project only needed columns.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>GroupBy with aggregates for reporting queries.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Queries are translated to SQL—check the generated SQL!</LessonTakeaway>
                </LessonTakeaways>
            </div>
